--- !u!114 &1697760397
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1697760396}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: d3316448f68d5f05bc3bd94a1e7091a0, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::EinsteinTelescope.Laser.LaserLevel
  title: Michelson
  briefing: 'Michelson interferometer: steer the two end test masses so both returning beams land in the centre of the output detector.'
  objectiveText: 'The laser beam is split in two by the <b>beam splitter</b>. Each half travels down an arm, bounces off an <b>end test mass</b> (a 62 cm mirror, the size planned for the Einstein Telescope) and comes back. Where the two returning beams meet is the <b>output port</b>: in a real detector, that is where a passing gravitational wave shows up.


    <b>Your task</b>

    Steer End mirror X and End mirror Y until both returning beams land inside the green circle of the output detector (within 1 cm) and keep them there for 2 seconds.


    <b>Stars</b>

    3 stars: within 2 minutes and at most 1 safety incident.   2 stars: within 4 minutes and at most 4.

    A safety incident is laser light hitting something it should not: a wall, a frame or the edge of an optic.'
  controlsTip: only the two end mirrors can be steered. Use COARSE steps to bring a beam onto the detector, then switch to FINE.
  physicsText: "<b>1. Law of reflection</b>\nA beam leaves a mirror at the same angle as it arrives:  angle in = angle out.\n\n<b>2. Tilt a mirror by \u03B8 and the beam turns by 2\u03B8</b>\nBoth angles change, so the turn is doubled. After a distance L the spot moves:\n            \u0394x \u2248 2 \xB7 \u03B8 \xB7 L          (\u03B8 in radians:  1\xB0 \u2248 0.0175 rad)\nEnd mirror X is L \u2248 3.7 m from the detector (2.5 m back to the splitter + 1.2 m to the detector). One COARSE step of 0.5\xB0 = 0.0087 rad moves the spot 2 \xD7 0.0087 \xD7 3.7 \u2248 6.5 cm. One FINE step moves it 6.5 mm. The goal is 1 cm, so the last part needs FINE steps.\n\n<b>3. The beam splitter</b>\nIt reflects half of the light and lets half through (50 / 50). The glass bends the light that goes through (Snell's law:  n1 \xB7 sin \u03B81 = n2 \xB7 sin \u03B82) and shifts it sideways by about 2.4 cm. That is why the detector is not exactly in the middle.\n\n<b>4. Why both beams must overlap</b>\nIn a real detector the two returning beams interfere. A gravitational wave changes the arm lengths by less than the width of a proton; only perfectly overlapping beams can show that."
  laser: {fileID: 1640499402}
  mirrors:
  - {fileID: 1891406447}
  - {fileID: 1903753273}
  checkpoints: []
  checkpointLabels: []
  checkpointHints: []
  detector: {fileID: 116673979}
